# {{app_name}} — Working Rules for AI Agents

This file is the canonical operating manual for any AI agent (Claude, GPT/Codex, Cursor, etc.) editing this Lazurite project. Read it before substantive work.

Mirrored verbatim at `AGENTS.md` for tooling that loads `AGENTS.md`.

---

## Write idiomatic Lazuli — read the lazuli-way index FIRST

Before authoring or editing any `.lzi` / `.lzx`, open **`knowledge/lazuli-way/0000-index.md`** — the idiom guide's *Fast path* + router. It carries the highest-frequency rules inline and points you to the **one** doctrine doc for the task at hand, so you load the index + 1 doc (not all of them). The `lazuli-architect` skill grounds its language facts there; so should you. When the fast-path doesn't settle it, open the doc it names; when even that doesn't, **ask the compiler** (`lazuli check` / `inspect` / `doctor`) — never guess or work around a rejected form.

---

## Quality gates — run before AND after every change

Lazuli is declarative. The compiler can catch most mistakes BEFORE they reach runtime — treat the CLI as your typecheck + lint:

```bash
pnpm lazuli:check         # schema validation (parser + analyzer + invariants)
pnpm lazuli:doctor        # strict-profile audit + per-layer coverage report
pnpm lazuli:test          # wire-thin handler test runner (`go test`)
pnpm verify:all           # full pre-merge gate (doctor + codegen + build + typecheck + tests)
```

The scaffolded `Lazurite.toml` ships with `[doctor] profile = "strict"` and `[doctor.coverage] preset = "tdd-strict"` — `handler_go` is enforced strictly (block_under = 90), other layers stay warn-only until you opt up to `preset = "tdd-mature"`. Per-layer `[doctor.coverage.<layer>]` sub-blocks override the preset when needed.

All four gates **MUST pass clean** before commit. Warnings should drop to zero or be explicitly suppressed with `# doctor:allow <RULE-CODE> — reason "..."` on the offending line (where the rule supports inline allow). To carry a baseline finding under `[doctor.test_discipline.severity_override."<RULE>"]`, the `reason` field is mandatory (DOCTOR-OVERRIDE-NEEDS-REASON-001).

When you can't immediately fix a doctor finding, track it (your own backlog, NOT this file) and continue — but flag it explicitly in the commit message.

## Validation harness (hooks)

This project ships a two-level, **agent-agnostic** validation harness in [`tools/lazuli-hooks/`](tools/lazuli-hooks/) so the gates above run **automatically**, not just when you remember to. The same scripts are wired for **both Claude Code** (`.claude/settings.json`) **and Codex** (`.codex/hooks.json`) — you don't invoke anything, it invokes itself:

- **Per-edit check (`PostToolUse`).** Every time you write or edit a `.lzi`/`.lzx` file (Claude `Write`/`Edit`/`MultiEdit`, Codex `apply_patch`), `lazuli check` runs on just that file. If it has a parse/analyzer **error**, the hook blocks the next step (stderr + exit 2) and shows you the diagnostic — so no broken source survives a single step. Warnings don't block; only real errors do.
- **Acceptance gate (`Stop`).** When you try to finish, `lazuli doctor . --security-profile strict --coverage --fail-on category:TestDiscipline` runs project-wide (every rule must have tests). If it's red, the hook blocks the stop with a reason — you cannot declare "done" with a red doctor. **Once you've written handler `.go` files**, the gate also generates + runs `lazuli test --layer handler --coverage --fail-on coverage:handler_go=90`, so coverage below the bar blocks the stop too (the coverage-forces-iterations mechanism). It auto-skips while there are no handlers yet; disable with `LAZULI_GATE_RUN_TESTS=0`, tune the bar with `LAZULI_GATE_HANDLER_COVERAGE`.

**Codex** needs a one-time trust step: run `/hooks` in the Codex TUI to approve the two hooks, and launch `codex` from the project root. Tune strictness via `Lazurite.toml` (`[doctor] profile`, `[doctor.coverage] preset`) — the hooks honor the manifest. To disable a level, remove its entry from `.claude/settings.json` / `.codex/hooks.json`. The scripts fail open (exit 0) if `lazuli` isn't on `PATH`, so a misconfigured machine never wedges editing. Full details + tuning: [`tools/lazuli-hooks/README.md`](tools/lazuli-hooks/README.md).

## Inspect for IR debugging

When behavior doesn't match expectation, ask the compiler what it actually sees:

```bash
# Full IR projection of a feature, pretty-printed
lazuli inspect features/<feature>/<feature>.lzi --format=lazuli --expand=all

# JSON for machine consumption
lazuli inspect features/<feature>/<feature>.lzi --format=json \
  --expand=summary,refs,events,policies,locators,dependencies,security

# Qualified symbol lookup
lazuli inspect <feature>.<resource>     # e.g. account.User
lazuli inspect <feature>.<query>        # e.g. catalog.list
```

Inspect shows derived references, computed scopes, security envelope, event flow, audit lineage — everything the compiler infers from your `.lzi`. Use when "I declared X but it doesn't behave like X" — inspect reveals what the compiler thinks X is.

